== 4.7 Chapter 4 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [Which of these groups of words is a clause?],
  [when the bell rang],
  [in the morning],
  [the tall boy],
  [to arrive early]
)

#mcq(2, [“Although the road was flooded, \_\_\_”],
  [and the bus could not pass],
  [so the bus could not pass],
  [the bus could not pass],
  [but the bus could not pass]
)

#mcq(3, [The book, \_\_\_ I bought last week, has been stolen.],
  [who],
  [that],
  [whose],
  [which]
)

#mcq(4, [The man \_\_\_ car was stolen has reported the matter to the police.],
  [whom],
  [who],
  [whose],
  [which]
)

#mcq(5, [Which sentence is punctuated correctly?],
  [The rain stopped and, we left at once.],
  [The rain stopped: we left, at once.],
  [The rain stopped; we left at once.],
  [The rain stopped, we left at once.]
)

#mcq(6, [The correct punctuation in “There was only one problem \_\_\_ the money had not arrived” is],
  [— ,],
  [:],
  [;],
  [,]
)

#mcq(7, [Which of these is a fragment?],
  [She left the room.],
  [Because the bus was late.],
  [The boys played football.],
  [The bell rang loudly.]
)

#mcq(8, [“It's tail was broken.” The correct form is],
  [Its tail was broken.],
  [It's' tail was broken.],
  [Its' tail was broken.],
  [It is tail was broken.]
)

#mcq(9, [The children's toys were scattered. The apostrophe shows],
  [a contraction],
  [a plural],
  [possession],
  [a missing letter]
)

#mcq(10, [Which of these words introduces a noun clause?],
  [because],
  [although],
  [that],
  [unless]
)

#mcq(11, [“When the bell rang, the students left the hall.” The first part is],
  [a dependent clause],
  [a sentence],
  [an independent clause],
  [a phrase]
)

#mcq(12, [Which sentence is complex?],
  [The rain stopped.],
  [Although the rain stopped, we stayed indoors.],
  [The rain stopped; we left.],
  [The rain stopped and we left.]
)

#mcq(13, [The commas in “My brother, who lives in Abuja, is a doctor” show that],
  [the sentence is compound],
  [the clause adds extra information],
  [the clause defines which brother],
  [the clause is a fragment]
)

#mcq(14, [Choose the correct sentence.],
  [Although he was tired, he finished the work.],
  [Although he was tired, but he finished the work.],
  [Although he was tired and he finished the work.],
  [Although he was tired, so he finished the work.]
)

#mcq(15, [The semicolon in “The road was flooded; however, we arrived early” is correct because],
  [however is an adverb joining two sentences],
  [the comma is optional],
  [however is a conjunction],
  [the sentence is compound-complex]
)

#mcq(16, [Which of these is a compound sentence?],
  [Although she sang, nobody listened.],
  [She sang and the audience applauded.],
  [Singing softly, she entered.],
  [She sang beautifully.]
)

#mcq(17, [“The man who came yesterday is my uncle.” The clause who came yesterday is],
  [an adverbial clause],
  [a noun clause],
  [non-defining],
  [defining]
)

#mcq(18, [Which sentence contains a noun clause?],
  [He left when the bell rang.],
  [Although he tried, he failed.],
  [The boy who won is my brother.],
  [What he said surprised everyone.]
)

#mcq(19, [The colon is correctly used in],
  [He brought three things; rice and beans.],
  [He brought three things: rice, beans and yam.],
  [He brought three things, rice and beans.],
  [He brought: rice and beans.]
)

#mcq(20, [Which of these needs a comma?],
  [The boy who came is here.],
  [He said that he would come.],
  [She bought bread and butter.],
  [If you hurry you will meet him.]
)

#mcq(21, [“The students who worked hard passed.” Removing the clause changes],
  [which students are meant],
  [the punctuation only],
  [the tense],
  [nothing]
)

#mcq(22, [The apostrophe in “the boys' books” is correct because],
  [the plural ends in -s],
  [books is plural],
  [it is a contraction],
  [boys is singular]
)

#mcq(23, [Which sentence is a run-on?],
  [The bell rang, the students left.],
  [The bell rang; the students left.],
  [When the bell rang, the students left.],
  [The bell rang. The students left.]
)

#mcq(24, [“Whom did you see?” The pronoun is correct because it is],
  [a possessive],
  [the subject of the verb],
  [the object of the verb],
  [a reflexive]
)

#mcq(25, [The dash in “He arrived late — as usual — and said nothing” is used to],
  [introduce a list],
  [mark an aside],
  [join two sentences],
  [show possession]
)

#mcq(26, [Which of these is a dependent clause?],
  [Unless you hurry.],
  [She sang.],
  [The sun rose.],
  [They arrived.]
)

#mcq(27, [“The news, which spread quickly, shocked the town.” The commas are],
  [wrong, because the clause is defining],
  [wrong, because news is plural],
  [optional, with no change of meaning],
  [correct, because the clause is non-defining]
)

#mcq(28, [The correct form is],
  [Its raining heavily.],
  [It's raining heavily.],
  [Its' raining heavily.],
  [It is raining heavily's.]
)

#mcq(29, [Which sentence is compound-complex?],
  [Singing well, she left.],
  [She sang well.],
  [She sang and the audience applauded.],
  [Although she sang well, the audience was small and nobody applauded.]
)

#mcq(30, [“He is the man \_\_\_ helped me.” The correct relative pronoun is],
  [which],
  [whose],
  [who],
  [whom]
)

#mcq(31, [Which of these sentences has no punctuation error?],
  [Its a long way to the station.],
  [Its' a long way to the station.],
  [It's a long way to the station.],
  [It is' a long way to the station.]
)

#mcq(32, [The comma in “After the meeting, we went home” is needed because],
  [the dependent clause comes first],
  [the sentence is compound],
  [home is an adverb],
  [we is a pronoun]
)

#mcq(33, [Which of these contains a relative clause?],
  [He ran quickly.],
  [The girl who won the prize is my sister.],
  [She bought a car.],
  [They will arrive tomorrow.]
)

#mcq(34, [“Although it rained, the match continued.” The sentence type is],
  [compound-complex],
  [compound],
  [simple],
  [complex]
)

#mcq(35, [The correct punctuation is “He asked \_\_\_ where are you going”],
  [!],
  [,],
  [?],
  [:]
)

#mcq(36, [Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon?],
  [I bought rice; I also bought beans.],
  [I bought rice; and beans.],
  [I bought rice; because beans were cheap.],
  [I bought; rice and beans.]
)

#mcq(37, [“The childrens' toys were broken.” The error is that],
  [toys should be possessive],
  [childrens is not a plural],
  [the sentence needs a semicolon],
  [children's needs no apostrophe]
)

#mcq(38, [Which of these is a phrase rather than a clause?],
  [after she arrived],
  [he left],
  [she arrived],
  [after her arrival]
)

#mcq(39, [The best repair of “He was tired, he went to bed” is],
  [He was tired, and, he went to bed.],
  [He was tired he went to bed.],
  [He was tired; he went to bed.],
  [He was tired: he went to bed and.]
)

#mcq(40, [“That is the house \_\_\_ roof was damaged.” The correct word is],
  [whom],
  [who],
  [which],
  [whose]
)

