#[
  #set text(size: 9.0pt)
  #set par(leading: 0.63em, spacing: 0.68em)

  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — when the bell rang has its own subject (the bell) and a finite verb (rang), so it is a clause. The other three are phrases: a prepositional phrase, a noun phrase and an infinitive phrase.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — although already marks the contrast, so the main clause follows with no conjunction at all. And, but and so would double the contrast, which is the error the question is built on.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — the clause is set off by commas, so it is non-defining, and a non-defining clause never takes that; it takes which. Who and whose cannot refer to a book.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — the clause needs a possessive, and whose supplies it. Who would replace the man rather than his car; which cannot refer to a person; whom is the object form and needs a verb of its own.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — two independent clauses joined by a semicolon, which is exactly the mark for that job. A is a comma splice; C misuses the colon and sets off an adverb; D separates a conjunction from its clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — the second part explains what the problem was, and a colon is the mark that introduces an explanation. A comma would be a splice, a semicolon would join two facts without marking the explanation, and the fourth option is not a single mark.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — because leaves the clause dependent, so there is no main clause and the group is a fragment. The other three each contain an independent clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    A — its is the possessive with no apostrophe. It is cannot precede a noun here, its' does not exist, and the fourth option doubles the error.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — the toys belong to the children, so the apostrophe marks possession. It does not stand for a missing letter, and the plural of children is already formed without it.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — that can introduce a noun clause acting as subject or object: I know that she is right. Although, because and unless introduce adverbial clauses of concession, reason and condition.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — when makes it dependent: it cannot stand alone. It has a subject and a verb, so it is not a phrase, and it is not a sentence because it has no main clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — a complex sentence contains one independent clause and at least one dependent clause, which although supplies. A is simple; B and D are compound.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — the commas mark a non-defining clause, which adds information about a brother already identified. A defining clause would take no commas, and the sentence is complex rather than compound.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — although is sufficient on its own, so the main clause follows with no conjunction. A doubles the contrast with but; C doubles it with so; D joins a dependent clause to a main clause with and, which distorts the meaning.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — however is a conjunctive adverb, so it needs a semicolon or a full stop before it. It is not a conjunction, which is why a comma alone would be a splice.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — two independent clauses joined by and. A is simple, C is complex, and D has a participial phrase rather than a second clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — there are no commas, and the clause tells you which man, so it is defining. A non-defining clause would take commas and comment on a man already identified.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — what he said acts as the subject of surprised, which is a noun's job. A and D contain adverbial clauses, and C contains a relative clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a colon introduces a list after a complete clause. A separates the verb from its object; C is a splice before a list; D uses a semicolon where a list follows rather than a clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a fronted dependent clause takes a comma after it: If you hurry, you will meet him. The others need no comma, and adding one to A would separate a verb from its object.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — the clause is defining, so removing it makes the sentence refer to all the students instead of the hard-working ones. That change of meaning is exactly why a defining clause takes no commas.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a plural already ending in -s takes the apostrophe alone. Boys is plural, not singular, and nothing has been omitted.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — two independent clauses joined by a comma alone is a comma splice, a kind of run-on. A uses a full stop, C a semicolon and D a subordinator, so all three are correct.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — whom is the object form, and it is the object of see. Who would be the subject, as in “who saw you?”.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a pair of dashes sets off an interruption or aside. Colons introduce lists, semicolons join clauses, and apostrophes show possession.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — unless leaves the clause waiting for a main clause, so it is dependent. The other three can stand alone.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — the clause adds information about news already identified, so it is non-defining and takes commas. Removing the commas would make it defining and change the meaning.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — it's is the contraction of it is, which is what the sentence means. Its is possessive, its' does not exist, and the fourth option is not English.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — two independent clauses (the audience was small, nobody applauded) plus a dependent clause (although she sang well). A is compound only, C simple, D a participial phrase.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — the pronoun is the subject of helped and refers to a person, so who. Which refers to things, whose is possessive, and whom is the object form.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — it's stands for it is, which is what the sentence needs. Its is possessive, its' does not exist, and the fourth option misplaces the apostrophe.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a fronted adverbial clause takes a comma after it. The other explanations have nothing to do with the rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — who won the prize describes the girl, which is a relative clause's job. The others contain no subordinate clause at all.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — one independent clause plus one dependent clause makes it complex. It is not compound, because there is only one clause that can stand alone.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a colon introduces the exact words of a question after a reporting clause. The question mark belongs at the end of the quoted words, and the comma would be a splice.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a semicolon joins two independent clauses. A puts it before a conjunction that does not need it, C separates a verb from its object, and D puts it before a dependent clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    A — the plural is children, not childrens, so the possessive is children's. The apostrophe itself is correctly doing a possessive job; the base form is wrong.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — after her arrival has a preposition and a noun but no finite verb, so it is a phrase. The others each contain a subject and a finite verb.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    B — a semicolon joins the two independent clauses correctly. A has no mark at all, C leaves a dangling and, and D misplaces the commas around the conjunction.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 15.47pt)[
    C — the clause needs a possessive, so whose, which works for things as well as people. Which would replace the house rather than its roof; who and whom refer to people.
  ]

]

